  :adr/id "adr-2608170400-portable-tests-are-not-portable"
  :adr/title "9% of portable suites run under ClojureScript, and the blocker is mostly two substitutions: 2,670 ExceptionInfo sites and System/exit"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-08-17"
  :source/dataset "adr"
  :adr/supersedes ""
  :adr/superseded-by ""
  :adr/context
  "ADR-2608170200 measured the JVM surface across 4,163 registered checkouts and
ADR-2608170300 built the gate that says whether a ClojureScript runner covers the suite
it sits in. Between them they produced a number that reads like a plan and is not one:
201 repositories have a ClojureScript runner, 181 of them complete, and another 2,034
hold a `deftest` in a `.cljc`/`.cljs` file with no `run-tests` call anywhere. About 9%
of portable suites are run by any ClojureScript host.

'2,034 repos need a runner' was the obvious reading. `kotoba-lang/nekko` showed it is
wrong. Adding a runner there covered ten of thirteen namespaces and failed on nine of
them until the TESTS were changed: they built key seeds with
`(byte-array (repeat 32 (byte 1)))`, and `byte-array` and `byte` are JVM-only. The
library under test was already properly portable -- `nekko.bytes/->ba` exists for exactly
this and is `#?(:clj (byte-array xs) :cljs (js/Uint8Array. (into-array xs)))`. So the
`.cljc` extension was load-bearing for the library and decorative for its tests.

That raised the question this ADR answers: across the 2,034, how much is a missing
runner and how much is a test that could never load under ClojureScript regardless?"

  :adr/decision
  "Report the split as a cost model rather than a single number, and report it as a RANGE
  where a single number would need reader-conditional parsing this measurement does not
  do:

    FLOOR   a test file uses a JVM-only token AND contains no `#?(` at all, so the token
            cannot be guarded. Definitely not portable.
    MIDDLE  a JVM-only token appears in a file that has some reader conditional, so it
            might be guarded.
    CLEAN   the tests use none of the tokens. A runner can be added today.

  `scripts/measure-test-portability.cljs` is landed so the numbers can be reproduced and
  re-run as the tranches close, but it is deliberately NOT registered in
  `manifest/orgs-detectors.edn`. The standing question -- does a runner cover its suite --
  already has `verify-cljs-runner-completeness`. A second detector emitting 2,034 per-repo
  findings that nobody would act on individually is the theatre this workspace keeps
  writing rules against; a script a human runs when planning a tranche is not."

  :adr/body
  "## The split (2026-08-17, 4,163 registered checkouts, 46s)

2,034 repos with portable tests and no ClojureScript runner, holding **5,653** portable
test files.

| class | repos | share |
|---|---|---|
| **CLEAN** — tests use no JVM-only token; a runner works today | **712** | 35% |
| MIDDLE — token present, possibly guarded | 1,134 | 56% |
| FLOOR — token present, no reader conditional at all | 169 | 8% |

(CLEAN was first reported as 731. The token list has since gained `format`,
`Long/`, `Integer/`, `Double/`, `Character/`, `BigDecimal`, `with-open` and
`Throwable` — each added because a sweep LOAD-FAIL named it — which moved 19
repos out of CLEAN.)

Test files: FLOOR 438, CEIL 2,202, of 5,653.

**So the single most useful fact is that 731 repositories need no portability work at
all.** They are the tranche to run first, and nothing in the earlier numbers said so.

## What actually blocks the rest, counted

Token frequency across the affected files:

| token | files | what it is |
|---|---|---|
| `System/` | 1,368 | see below |
| `clojure.lang.*` | 622 | see below |
| `java.*` package refs | 548 | |
| `clojure.java.*` | 373 | io / shell |
| `byte-array` | 47 | nekko's blocker — rare |
| `(:import …)` | 24 | |

Broken down by actual member, over portable files workspace-wide:

    clojure.lang.ExceptionInfo   2670        System/exit              1882
    clojure.lang.ArityException    16        System/getProperty       1001
    clojure.lang.PersistentQueue    8        System/getenv             840
    clojure.lang.BigInt             7        System/currentTimeMillis  456
                                             System/nanoTime           163
                                             System/arraycopy           98

Two conclusions the aggregate hid:

**`clojure.lang.ExceptionInfo` is 2,670 of the 2,701 `clojure.lang` uses** — 99%. It is
the `(is (thrown-with-msg? clojure.lang.ExceptionInfo #\"…\" …))` idiom, and the portable
form is already in use in this workspace: `kotoba_rad/canonical_test.cljc` writes
`#?(:clj Exception :cljs js/Error)` while its near-twin `nekko/canonical_test.cljc` wrote
the JVM class. Two copies of one assertion, one portable — drift, not design. One
substitution.

**`System/exit` is 1,882 and is runner plumbing, not test logic** — the exit-code hook at
the end of a test entry. The portable form is
`#?(:clj (System/exit n) :cljs (set! (.-exitCode js/process) n))`, which several runners
in this workspace already use. Also one substitution.

`getProperty` / `getenv` / `currentTimeMillis` / `nanoTime` (2,460 combined) are the part
that needs a small portable helper rather than a substitution, and `arraycopy` (98) and
the `java.*` refs are the genuinely per-site work.

## The third category: tests that pass on the JVM because the shim is identity there

Running the tranche found a blocker no token scan can reach. `cloud-itonami/media`
and `gftdcojp/mamori` both carry, verbatim:

    (defn- ->clj [x] #?(:cljs (js->clj x :keywordize-keys true) :clj x))
    (defn- ->js  [x] #?(:cljs (clj->js x) :clj x))

Their public functions take a parameter literally named `js-obj` and return
`(->js …)`: this is a JS-object boundary API for a Worker. On the JVM both
conversions are the identity, so tests written as
`(:valid (core/validate-subject {…}))` pass. Under ClojureScript — the platform
the code is actually deployed on — the result is a `#js {…}` and keyword lookup
returns nil. 5 failures in media, 11 in mamori.

**The tests are written for the platform the code is not deployed on**, and
nothing in the source is JVM-only. Only running it shows this.

Measured: exactly **3** repositories carry that shim (`media`, `mamori`, and
`news`, whose tests happen not to touch the boundary functions and therefore
pass). So the category is real and narrow, and does not change the tranche plan.

## The first tranche, measured rather than projected

| | first filters | corrected filters |
|---|---|---|
| CLEAN and self-contained | 37 | **322** |
| verified PASS | 7 (19%) | **~88%** |

The candidate count went UP because the first dependency filter was too strict,
not too loose: nearly every repo here pins `io.github.cognitect-labs/test-runner`
as a GIT dep, and counting that as a cross-repo dependency excluded almost
everything. It is irrelevant to an nbb run. In the other direction the filter had
ignored maven entirely, which is why 20 of the first 37 candidates died on
LOAD-FAIL against `cheshire.core` and friends.

`scripts/list-portable-runner-candidates.cljs`,
`scripts/sweep-portable-runners.cljs` and `scripts/land-portable-runners.cljs`
are the three steps, landed so the remaining ~390 CLEAN-but-not-self-contained
repositories can be worked the same way once their classpath question is settled.

## Why this is not 'just run sed'

The two dominant patterns are mechanically substitutable, but a substitution that has not
been run is not a fix. nekko is the worked example and it took three rounds:
`byte-array` -> `nb/->ba` made nine namespaces load; the ExceptionInfo substitution made a
tenth pass; and three namespaces still cannot run under nbb for a reason no substitution
reaches -- `chain.core` encodes `prev` as an `ipld.core/Link` deftype whose field sci
cannot read, which is why that repo has a second, compiled-cljs host. A mass edit would
have produced 2,670 changed files and an unknown number of newly-red suites.

The order that worked, and that the 731 make cheap:

  1. CLEAN repos: add a runner, run it, land it. No source edits.
  2. ExceptionInfo sites: substitute, run, land per repo.
  3. `System/exit` in runners: substitute, run, land per repo.
  4. env/clock access: introduce a portable helper, then migrate.
  5. What remains after that is real per-site work and should be sized then, not now.

## What is NOT claimed

The FLOOR/MIDDLE boundary is a bound, not a fact: deciding whether a token sits inside a
`#?(:clj …)` branch needs paren-balanced reader-conditional parsing, which this
measurement does not do. nekko's nine files were in FLOOR, so the class is real, but the
1,134 MIDDLE repos are genuinely unknown until someone runs them.

Nothing here says the 731 CLEAN repos' tests PASS under ClojureScript -- only that they
can load. nekko's own experience says some will fail on first run, and those failures are
the point of running them.

The share of `.cljc` that is decorative rather than load-bearing is now a measured
question, not a suspicion, and the answer is that it is common enough that 'portable' in
a filename should not be trusted without a run."

  :adr/consequences
  "- The migration has a costed first tranche: 731 repositories where a ClojureScript
  runner is pure addition. Before this, the 2,034 were undifferentiated.
- The two largest blockers are named with counts and with the portable form each already
  has somewhere in this workspace, so neither needs a design decision.
- `verify-cljs-runner-completeness` prints the 2,034 as a counted class, so the 9% stays
  visible; this ADR is what makes it actionable.
- `scripts/measure-test-portability.cljs` is landed and re-runnable, but NOT registered
  as a detector: the standing question is already covered, and 2,034 per-repo findings
  nobody would act on individually is the failure mode ADR-2608124800 catalogued.
- nekko itself: ten of thirteen namespaces landed (dd613e4, 49 tests / 115 assertions
  under nbb, JVM unchanged at 84 / 172). The remaining three belong in its compiled host
  and are NOT landed -- the repo-wide build slot was held by other sessions through four
  attempts, and an unverified change to a test runner is exactly what this line of work
  exists to prevent."

